`EmulatorFeatureFlags` is documented as thread-safe, and its `flags_` member is
annotated with `ABSL_GUARDED_BY(mu_)`. However, `flags()` returns a reference to
`flags_` after its local reader lock has been released:

```cpp
const Flags& flags() const ABSL_LOCKS_EXCLUDED(mu_) {
  absl::ReaderMutexLock l(&mu_);
  return flags_;
}
```

The lock is destroyed when `flags()` returns, before the caller reads through
the returned reference. A concurrent `set_flags()` call can therefore modify
the same object while that reference is being read.

I reproduced this with both Clang Thread Safety Analysis and ThreadSanitizer at
the current `master` revision,
`2abe04c1ace40b8760133e6ca0800d257d127da4`.

### Static diagnostic

Building the real `//backend/locking:manager` target with Clang 18 thread-safety
analysis reports one project-owned reference-return diagnostic:

```text
./common/feature_flags.h:68:12: warning: returning variable 'flags_' by
reference requires holding mutex 'mu_' [-Wthread-safety-reference-return]
   68 |     return flags_;
      |            ^
```

The relevant build invocation was equivalent to:

```shell
CC=/usr/bin/clang-18 CXX=/usr/bin/clang++-18 \
  bazel build //backend/locking:manager \
  --check_direct_dependencies=off \
  --action_env=CC=/usr/bin/clang-18 \
  --action_env=CXX=/usr/bin/clang++-18 \
  --repo_env=CC=/usr/bin/clang-18 \
  --repo_env=CXX=/usr/bin/clang++-18 \
  --copt=-Wthread-safety
```

The issue is the lifetime relationship rather than a missing lock acquisition:
the getter does acquire `mu_`, but the returned alias outlives that lock.

### ThreadSanitizer reproduction

I added the following temporary probe. It is only a reproducer, not part of the
proposed project change.

<details>
<summary><code>common/feature_flags_tsan_probe.cc</code></summary>

```cpp
#include "common/feature_flags.h"

#include <atomic>
#include <cstdint>
#include <thread>

namespace emulator = ::google::spanner::emulator;

int main() {
  auto& mutable_flags = const_cast<emulator::EmulatorFeatureFlags&>(
      emulator::EmulatorFeatureFlags::instance());
  std::atomic<bool> start = false;
  std::atomic<bool> stop = false;
  std::atomic<std::uint64_t> observations = 0;

  std::thread writer([&] {
    while (!start.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) {
    }
    emulator::EmulatorFeatureFlags::Flags flags;
    for (int i = 0; i < 500000; ++i) {
      const bool value = (i & 1) != 0;
      flags.enable_check_constraint = value;
      flags.enable_column_default_values = value;
      flags.enable_protos = value;
      flags.enable_dml_returning = value;
      mutable_flags.set_flags(flags);
    }
    stop.store(true, std::memory_order_release);
  });

  std::thread reader([&] {
    start.store(true, std::memory_order_release);
    std::uint64_t local = 0;
    while (!stop.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) {
      const auto& flags = emulator::EmulatorFeatureFlags::instance().flags();
      local += flags.enable_check_constraint;
      local += flags.enable_column_default_values;
      local += flags.enable_protos;
      local += flags.enable_dml_returning;
    }
    observations.store(local, std::memory_order_relaxed);
  });

  writer.join();
  reader.join();
  return observations.load(std::memory_order_relaxed) == UINT64_MAX;
}
```

</details>

The corresponding temporary Bazel target is:

```starlark
load("@rules_cc//cc:cc_binary.bzl", "cc_binary")

cc_binary(
    name = "feature_flags_tsan_probe",
    srcs = ["feature_flags_tsan_probe.cc"],
    linkopts = ["-pthread"],
    deps = [":feature_flags"],
)
```

I built and ran it with:

```shell
bazel build //common:feature_flags_tsan_probe \
  --check_direct_dependencies=off \
  --copt=-O1 --copt=-g \
  --copt=-fsanitize=thread \
  --linkopt=-fsanitize=thread

TSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1:exitcode=66 \
  ./bazel-bin/common/feature_flags_tsan_probe
```

The mutation path mirrors the existing
`ScopedEmulatorFeatureFlagsSetter`, which also obtains the singleton through
`const_cast` and invokes `set_flags()`.

With the current getter, the probe exits with the configured TSAN failure code
(`66`) and reports:

```text
WARNING: ThreadSanitizer: data race
  Write of size 1 ... by thread T1 (mutexes: write M0)
  Previous read of size 1 ... by thread T2
  Location is heap block ... allocated by
  google::spanner::emulator::EmulatorFeatureFlags::instance()
```

### Proposed repair

Returning a snapshot by value keeps the copy inside the reader-lock scope:

```diff
-  const Flags& flags() const ABSL_LOCKS_EXCLUDED(mu_) {
+  Flags flags() const ABSL_LOCKS_EXCLUDED(mu_) {
     absl::ReaderMutexLock l(&mu_);
     return flags_;
   }
```

After this one-line change:

- the same TSAN probe exits `0` with no data-race report; and
- a focused Clang 18 build passes with both `-Wthread-safety` and
  `-Werror=thread-safety-reference-return`.

The current call-site forms that immediately inspect one field, or initialize a
local `Flags` value, remain source-compatible and instead receive a consistent
snapshot.

### Impact and validation boundary

This demonstrates an API-level concurrency correctness problem in a class that
explicitly promises thread safety. I have not established that a production
emulator workload mutates these flags concurrently. In the current repository,
`set_flags()` appears to be used primarily through test support, so I do not
want to overstate the observed production impact.


Would returning `Flags` by value be an acceptable fix here, or is there a
preferred accessor pattern for this singleton?

It looks like there's a leak in the emulator - If you do a bunch of reads, but use new connections, the emulator gets slower and slower to respond, regardless of what your queries are.

Deleting rows, dropping tables, deleting the database, and deleting the instance has no effect on performance after that - Only stopping the process causes it to get speedy again.

Below is an example of a naïve test case - Running this results in 10-20ms additional latency per query per 100 connections, with no upper bound until your machine runs out of memory. Moving the client creation to have the connection persist removes the impact on latency.

Some incompetent digging in some core files shows these two symbols as possibly notable:
